  "hypothesis": "after group averaging the blind optimum needs only the ordinary scalar and the width-q off-query",
  "test": "EXISTS one mixture theta over the two SURVIVORS (ordinary scalar, width-q off-query), the SAME mixture for every alternative, with sum_s theta_s Ibar_s(a,nu) >= Ibar_c(a,nu) FORALL alternatives a and ALL nu in [0,1]. theta is searched by an LP on a nu grid and then VERIFIED off-grid and optimizer-free: the margin is quadratic in nu and its minimum on [0,1] is at an endpoint or the vertex",
  "quantifier_order_correction": "the previous version chose the best survivor SEPARATELY FOR EACH ALTERNATIVE, i.e. it tested FORALL a EXISTS s FORALL nu, which is strictly weaker and cannot justify moving allocation mass. counterexample_control() exhibits I_s1=(1,0), I_s2=(0,1), I_c=(3/4,3/4): the weak test passes, both correct tests reject, and deleting c lowers the minimax value from 3/4 to 1/2. That refutes the TEST, not the D6 conjecture.",
  "test_design_correction": "an earlier version asked whether the width-q class dominates the ordinary scalar too. It does not, and it should not: the scalar is one of the two survivors, and the failures appeared exactly at mu = 0.75, which is ABOVE the phase boundary mu*(q) where the scalar is supposed to enter. The wrong test was reporting the mechanism as a refutation.",
